course: language
lesson: 50
title: Not unhappy and not half: litotes
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Litotes (say it LIE-toe-teez) is a special kind of understatement: you say something good by saying «not» plus its opposite. «I'm not unhappy with that» means «I'm happy»; «not too shabby» means «rather good».
explain: Picture Mum reading Jack's school report, full of «very good» and «excellent», and saying with a little smile: «Well, I'm not unhappy with that». She is pleased, but saying it the long way round sounds modest and a bit funny.
explain: A useful trick: two «nots» often cancel out, like a step back and a step back again bringing you to where you began. «Not unhappy» is happy; «no small thing» is a big thing; «not a million miles away» is close.
explain: But watch out: some of these phrases are put-downs dressed up softly («he's not the brightest»), and a few sound like «no» but mean a big yes («Not half!»).
fact: Litotes is understatement made with «not» or «no» plus the opposite word: «not bad» for good, «not unhappy» for happy, «no small thing» for a big thing.
fact: «Well, I'm not unhappy with that» means «I'm pleased»; the two negatives (not, un-) cancel out.
fact: «That's not the worst idea you've had» is a dry way of saying «good idea»; a smile and «let's do it» show what Dad really means.
fact: «He's not the brightest» means «he isn't clever»; playing it down makes it sound gentle, but it is still a put-down, so it is not kind to use about classmates.
fact: «It's not exactly warm in here» means «it's cold»; «not exactly» plus a word often means the opposite of that word.
fact: «Not too shabby» means rather good; «shabby» on its own means worn out and tatty.
fact: «She's no stranger to trouble» means she knows trouble very well, because she often gets into it.
fact: «Did I enjoy it? Not half!» is old British slang for «yes, very much»; it sounds like a no but means a big yes.
fact: «I wouldn't say no to a biscuit» is a polite yes, so pass the biscuits.
fact: «You're not a million miles away» means you are close: a guess of 80 when the jar holds 85 is close.
fact: «That was no small thing» means it was a big and brave thing to do; it is praise.
exercise: Mum reads Jack's report, full of «very good» and «excellent»: «Well, I'm not unhappy with that». How does she feel? | Pleased | Sad | Angry | Worried
exercise: Why does «not unhappy» mean happy? | The two negatives cancel each other out | «Not» makes feelings bigger | «Unhappy» is a kind word for very happy | Mums always mean the opposite of what they say
exercise: Tia suggests a picnic for her birthday. Dad says «That's not the worst idea you've had». What does he think? | It's a good idea | It's a bad idea but not the very worst | It's the worst idea ever | He isn't sure
exercise: At the bus stop: «Bless him, he's not the brightest». What do they mean about the man? | He isn't very clever | He has a dim torch with him | He is very clever | He is very kind
exercise: Why shouldn't you say «he's not the brightest» about a classmate? | It sounds gentle but it's still a put-down | It's a compliment, and he might get big-headed | It's a word only teachers are allowed to use | It means he has done something very wrong
exercise: In a freezing sports hall: «It's not exactly warm in here, is it». What does it mean? | It's cold in here | It's nearly warm | It's too hot | It's exactly right
exercise: Callum's mum looks at his drawing: «Ooh, not too shabby!». What does she think? | It's rather good | It's worn out and tatty | It needs more colour | It's too messy to keep
exercise: What does «shabby» mean on its own? | Worn out and tatty | Shiny and new | Very bright and colourful | Quite small
exercise: Grandad watches his granddaughter climb the tree again: «She's no stranger to trouble, that one». What does he mean? | She often gets into trouble | She has never been in trouble | She doesn't know what trouble is | She is a stranger in the town
exercise: In Manchester: «Did I enjoy the match? Not half!». What does he mean? | Yes, very much | No, not at all | Only for half the match | He left at half time
exercise: Gran says «Well, I wouldn't say no to a biscuit». What should you do? | Pass her a biscuit | Wait for a yes | Take the biscuits away | Ask her again three times
exercise: Ella guesses 80 sweets. The jar has 85. «Ooh, you're not a million miles away». What does it mean? | She's very close | She's very far off | She's exactly right | She's a million out
exercise: The teacher says to shy Sam after the play: «That was no small thing, Sam». What does she mean? | It was a big, brave thing to do | It was a small and unimportant thing | He was too small to see | He should do a bigger part next time
exercise: Which of these is litotes? | «Not too shabby» | «I'm starving» | «Best ever» | «A million times»
exercise: Which of these sounds like a no but means a big yes? | «Not half!» | «Not exactly» | «Not ideal» | «Not today»
test: «my mum said she's not unhappy with my report, so she's sad, right??» What is right? | No, «not unhappy» means she's pleased | Yes, «unhappy» is in it, so she's sad | Yes, «not» always means the opposite of good | No, it means she hasn't read it yet
test: Tia hears «That's not the worst idea you've had» and sulks: «so it's a bad idea». Then Dad gets the picnic basket out. What has she missed? | It was a dry way of saying «good idea» | Dad is going on a picnic without her | Dad means it's only the second worst idea | Dad was talking about another idea
test: ★ Your friend calls a boy in your class «not the brightest, bless him» and says «it's fine, I said it nicely». What is true? | Saying it softly doesn't stop it being a put-down | It's fine, because «bless him» turns it into praise | It's fine, because it means he's clever | It means the boy needs a brighter lamp
test: «they said 'not half' about the match so they only liked half of it lol» What is right? | «Not half!» is old slang for «yes, very much» | They only watched the first half of the match | They liked less than half of the players | They wanted all their money back for the second half
test: You offer Gran a biscuit and she says «I wouldn't say no». You put the tin away because «she didn't say yes». What went wrong? | «I wouldn't say no» was a polite yes | Gran really wanted you to put them away | Gran only eats biscuits on Sundays | Gran said no in a very polite way
test: ★ In a guessing game the answer is 85. Who is «not a million miles away»? | Ben, who guessed 82 | Mia, who guessed 30 | Leo, who guessed 1,000,000 | Zoe, who guessed 300
test: ★★ «Not bad», «not unhappy», «not too shabby», «no small thing». What do they all have in common? | They say something good by saying «not» plus its opposite | They all mean that the speaker is quite disappointed | They are all hyperbole that makes things bigger | They are all sarcasm that means the opposite
test: ★★ A cartoon shows a man reading «she's no stranger to trouble» and thinking the girl has never met trouble. What is the right meaning? | She knows trouble very well, because she's often in it | She has never once been in any trouble at all | She is new in town and doesn't know anyone | Trouble is the name of her best friend
test: ★★★ Dad says «It's not unlikely that it won't rain». Count the «not»-type words: not, un-, won't. Does he think it will rain? | No: three negatives leave one, so he thinks it probably won't rain | Yes: three negatives always mean a big yes | Yes: the word «rain» is in it, so it will rain | Nobody can ever work out a sentence with three negatives
test: ★★★ Gran says «It's not exactly warm» in the sports hall, and later «It's not exactly cold» by the fire. Where is it colder? | The sports hall | By the fire | Both are the same | Neither is cold
exam: Identify the figure of speech employed in the utterance «I'm not unhappy with that», made by a parent upon reading an excellent report. | Litotes, an affirmation expressed by the negation of its opposite | Hyperbole, an exaggeration for effect | Sarcasm, an expression of the opposite meaning in mockery | A literal statement of indifference
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the British expression «Not half!», given in reply to an enquiry whether the speaker enjoyed a match. | Emphatic agreement: yes, very much | Partial enjoyment confined to one half of the match | Complete disapproval of the match | Departure at half time
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the remark «he's not the brightest», made concerning a third party? | A softened expression which nonetheless constitutes a disparagement | A compliment upon the person's intelligence | A comment concerning the person's lamp | A neutral observation without any judgement
exam: It is given that a pupil guessed 80 and that the true number was 85. Select the response consistent with the expression «You're not a million miles away». | The guess was close | The guess was distant | The guess was exact | The guess exceeded a million
